Investigate a cart with motion, momentum and energy (@physics-foundation-study: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Review-systems-motion-and-physical-evidence.lingua#r_0B390092C821608D642BF70359") { r_005C89AACDDC8AD7217B4BF754
Bring the foundation together with a hypothetical 3 kg cart on a horizontal track. Use an inertial-frame approximation, a fixed positive direction, initial rest, an 8 N constant pull and a 2 N constant opposing resistance while it moves forward. Neglect rotation and deformation for the first translational model. These supplied values are teaching assumptions, not measurements of an installed system.

Before calculating, draw the cart boundary and identify the quantities required to test the model. State which vertical forces balance and which horizontal forces determine acceleration. Explain why the hand/cart action-reaction pair does not cancel the pull in the isolated cart's horizontal balance. Specify the origin, time zero and point used for position observations.

Predict position, velocity, momentum and kinetic energy at 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5 and 2 s. Derive the numerical formulas x = t² metres, v = 2t m/s, p = 6t kg m/s and K = 6t² joules, with t expressed in seconds. Check the final 4 m, 4 m/s, 12 kg m/s and 24 J independently using graph areas, impulse and work. Track pulling work, resistance work and net work separately.

At 2 s, remove the pull and assume the 2 N resistance remains opposite the forward motion until rest. The acceleration during this stopping interval is −2/3 m/s². The cart takes a further 6 s to stop and travels a further 12 m, reaching x = 16 m at total elapsed time 8 s. Its 24 J of translational kinetic energy is removed by −24 J of resistance work. Once velocity reaches zero, do not extend that same signed resistance formula into spontaneous backward motion: the assumed resistance direction and the model's interval of validity must be reconsidered.

Now add observational questions. How often must position be sampled to distinguish the pulling and stopping intervals? What uncertainty in position or time would matter to the inferred acceleration? Could a change in resistance or a timing offset produce a similar residual? Design a benign observation or use a supplied numerical simulation; do not claim an experiment was performed when the work is analytical.

**Evidence:** present the boundary diagram, source of each input, piecewise model, checked table, momentum and energy accounts, a changed-assumption comparison and a criticism of what the particle approximation omits. Explain how a broader boundary changes the energy inventory without changing the requirement for a consistent physical account.

**Continue:** [Review systems, motion and physical evidence](Review-systems-motion-and-physical-evidence.lingua#r_0B390092C821608D642BF70359).

